# Project: Rekreate Lead Intelligence Engine

## What this is
An automated B2B lead discovery and technical-audit pipeline for Rekreate Digital,
a Manila-based agency that builds and runs custom AI agents and web platforms for
clients ("we build it, you own it, we run it").

The engine finds local service businesses on Google Maps, audits their public web
infrastructure for conversion gaps, scores them against an ICP, and drafts an
outbound hook grounded in the specific gaps found.

First target segment: property management companies in Greater Philadelphia.
That segment is chosen because Rekreate has a matching case study — Two River
Development, a Philadelphia affordable-housing company for whom Rekreate built an
operations platform and public website as one system.

## Tech stack (do not substitute without asking)
- TypeScript, Node 20+, ESM
- Postgres (Supabase or local docker) — raw SQL via `postgres` or `pg`. No ORM.
- `zod` for validating every external response before it enters the system
- `vitest` for tests
- `commander` for the CLI
- Anthropic SDK for the pitch stage
- No web framework, no UI, no queue system in v1

## Hard constraints — violating any of these is a bug, not a style choice

1. **Google Text Search caps at 60 results per query, 20 per page, 3 pages max.**
   A single query never represents a market. All discovery MUST use recursive
   rectangle tiling: search a bbox, and when it returns 60 results, split it into
   four quadrants and search each. Recurse until tiles return under 60 or max
   depth is reached. Dedupe on `place_id`.

2. **Use Places API (New) only** — `POST https://places.googleapis.com/v1/places:searchText`.
   The legacy Places API cannot be enabled on new GCP projects.

3. **The field mask is fixed.** Exactly:
   `places.id, places.displayName, places.formattedAddress, places.nationalPhoneNumber,
    places.websiteUri, places.rating, places.userRatingCount, places.businessStatus,
    places.primaryType, places.types, places.location, nextPageToken`
   Places API (New) bills at the highest tier among requested fields. This mask is
   Enterprise (~$35 per 1,000 calls). Adding `places.reviews` or `places.photos`
   re-prices every call at ~$40 per 1,000 for data this project does not use.
   Never add fields to this mask without an explicit instruction.

4. **Never make Place Details calls for enrichment.** Phone, website, rating and
   review count all come back in the search call. A per-result Details call
   multiplies cost by ~20x for identical data. The only permitted Details call is
   an ID-only refresh, which is free.

5. **Storage rules follow Google's terms, and they are not optional.**
   - `place_id` may be stored indefinitely. It is the primary key.
   - All other Google-sourced fields (name, address, phone, rating, review count)
     are a cache. Every row carries `google_refreshed_at`. Anything older than 30
     days is stale and must be re-fetched or excluded from exports.
   - Data derived from the prospect's OWN website (the audit), and data this
     project generates (scores, gaps, pitch hooks, outreach state), is Rekreate's
     own and has no TTL.
   - Design the schema so the Google cache and the derived data are separate
     tables. Never denormalise them into one wide table.

6. **Hard cost ceiling on every run.** A configurable `maxCalls` that halts the
   sweep, plus estimated USD cost logged at the end of every run. A runaway
   recursion must be impossible, not unlikely.

7. **The LLM never sees raw HTML.** The audit stage produces a structured `gaps`
   array. The pitch stage takes only `{ name, rating, reviewCount, gaps }`. This
   is what stops the model inventing problems the prospect does not have.

8. **The LLM only runs on leads above the score threshold.** Scoring is
   deterministic and free; generation is neither.

9. **Scoring is pure functions.** `score(lead) -> { score, tier, reasons, gaps }`
   with no I/O, no clock reads, no randomness. It must be unit-testable with
   fixtures and it must be trivial to change weights without touching the pipeline.

10. **No email sending in v1.** The pipeline ends at a reviewed export. Outbound
    sending is a separate decision with its own domain and warm-up.

11. **Idempotent runs.** Re-running the same sweep updates rows, never duplicates
    them. Upsert on `place_id`.

12. **Secrets live in `.env`, which is gitignored.** Commit `.env.example` with
    keys and empty values. Never write a key into source, a test, or a comment.

## Verify before you write
Before writing any Places API request, read the current Text Search (New)
reference at
https://developers.google.com/maps/documentation/places/web-service/text-search
and confirm the request body shape, the `locationRestriction.rectangle` format,
and the pagination fields. Do not rely on memory of this API — it changed shape
in 2025 and older patterns are wrong.

Same for the Anthropic SDK: check the current model identifiers rather than
assuming. The blueprint this project came from specifies "Claude 3.5 Sonnet",
which is outdated.

## Working style
- Propose a plan before writing code for any phase. Wait for approval.
- One phase at a time. Do not scaffold ahead.
- Ask before adding any dependency not listed above.
- Never write mock or fallback data that lets a broken API call look successful.
  A failed request should fail loudly with the status and body.
- Every module gets tests before the phase is considered done.
